      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Until now, red wine has been the cardioprotective beverage of choice, but the convenience and lack of adverse effects make beer a more cost-effective, safe and widely available choice. A paucity of evidence in support of this led to our study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "During a 6-month period, 11,780 consecutive patrons at 18 randomly selected urban and community liquor distributors were recruited. Participants were randomly assigned to receive either beer or wine daily for 5 years. Of the 10,078 people who met the inclusion criteria, 5033 were assigned to receive red wine and 5039 to receive beer every day."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Effectiveness and adverse effects were equal in the 2 groups. However, costs for beer were substantially lower."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "INTERPRETATION",
          "content": "In view of its effectiveness, low cost, acceptable adverse-event profile and the fact that it is available in convenient single-dose dispensers, beer should replace red wine as the cardioprotective beverage of choice."
        }
      ]},
